From: Tim Douglas <me@timdoug.com>
Subject: [PATCH] posix_madvise: check the range for POSIX_MADV_DONTNEED too

POSIX_MADV_DONTNEED returned 0 for any range, where POSIX requires
EINVAL for a misaligned or impossible one and ENOMEM for one not mapped.
Linux's MADV_DONTNEED discards pages, which POSIX's must not, so the
range is checked with MADV_NORMAL, which changes nothing.

Signed-off-by: Tim Douglas <me@timdoug.com>

diff --git a/src/mman/posix_madvise.c b/src/mman/posix_madvise.c
index e5e5acb..be80f7d 100644
--- a/src/mman/posix_madvise.c
+++ b/src/mman/posix_madvise.c
@@ -4,6 +4,8 @@
 
 int posix_madvise(void *addr, size_t len, int advice)
 {
-	if (advice == MADV_DONTNEED) return 0;
+	/* POSIX's DONTNEED is a hint; Linux's discards the pages. Its range
+	 * is still checked, with a hint that changes nothing. */
+	if (advice == MADV_DONTNEED) advice = MADV_NORMAL;
 	return -__syscall(SYS_madvise, addr, len, advice);
 }
